I wrote `polyglot/main.rs`, and it builds and runs under both `rustc` and `g++ -x c++`. Both binaries printed the same answers for N = 0, 1, 2, 5, 10, 50 and 90 (1, 1, 2, 8, 89, 20365011074, 4660046610375530309).

When I compiled with `rustc`, I added `-o polyglot/main` to put the binary next to the source. Without `-o`, `rustc` writes `main` into the directory you run it from. So your command only finds `polyglot/main` if you run it from inside `polyglot/`, or if you add `-o`.

```rust
/* /* */
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    long n = argc > 1 ? atol(argv[1]) : 0;
    unsigned long long a = 1, b = 1;
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) { unsigned long long t = a + b; a = b; b = t; }
    printf("%llu\n", a);
    return 0;
}
#if 0
// */
fn main() {
    let n: u64 = std::env::args().nth(1).and_then(|s| s.trim().parse().ok()).unwrap_or(0);
    let (mut a, mut b): (u64, u64) = (1, 1);
    for _ in 0..n { let t = a.wrapping_add(b); a = b; b = t; }
    println!("{}", a);
}
/* /* */
#endif
// */
```

**How it works:** Rust lets block comments nest, and C++ doesn't.
- **First line, `/* /* */`:** In C++ this is one complete comment. In Rust the `*/` closes only the inner `/*`, so the outer comment is still open. Rust therefore treats the C++ code that follows as comment text.
- **`// */`:** Inside a block comment, Rust doesn't treat `//` specially, so this `*/` closes the open comment. C++ sees a normal line comment.
- **`#if 0` … `#endif`:** C++ skips the Rust code between these lines. The closing lines repeat the same comment trick so that Rust never sees the `#endif`.

**Limit:** Both versions use 64-bit unsigned integers, so results are correct up to N = 92. Beyond that the numbers wrap around.
